---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const functionToolCode = `from google.adk.agents import Agent

def check_machine_status(machine_id: str) -> dict:
    """Return current alarm, mode, and last-seen timestamp."""
    status = mes_client.get_machine(machine_id)
    return dict(
        status="success",
        alarm=status.alarm_code,
        mode=status.mode,
        last_seen=status.last_seen,
    )

root_agent = Agent(
    model="gemini-2.5-flash",
    name="maintenance_agent",
    instruction="Inspect machine state before recommending actions.",
    tools=[check_machine_status],
)`;
---

<SiteLayout current="function-tools" title="Function tools">
  <section class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Tool form 03A</p>
      <h1>Function tools turn intent into typed actions.</h1>
      <p>
        They are the narrowest useful tool form: a named operation, a schema,
        a predictable result, and a clear failure mode.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="practice-section practice-section--do">
        <span class="tool-kind">Use when</span>
        <h3>The action is bounded.</h3>
        <p>
          The model should choose parameters, not invent the implementation.
          Good examples: create ticket, check inventory, validate serial number,
          calculate quote, or fetch machine status.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="practice-section practice-section--do">
        <span class="tool-kind">Design for</span>
        <h3>Contracts and recovery.</h3>
        <p>
          Keep inputs typed, outputs structured, errors explicit, and side
          effects idempotent. A failed tool call should become useful context
          for the next decision.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="practice-section practice-section--avoid">
        <span class="tool-kind">Avoid</span>
        <h3>Generic escape hatches.</h3>
        <p>
          A function named <code>do_task</code> with a raw prompt argument is
          not a contract. Split it into smaller operations the system can test,
          trace, retry, and permission.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="section-head compact-head" style="margin-top: 40px;">
      <p class="eyebrow">The protocol</p>
      <h2>How a function tool call actually flows.</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="timeline-list">
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">01</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Setup</strong>
          <p>The runtime introspects the Python signature and docstring into a JSON schema, then hands the schema to the model as part of its prompt. The model sees a list of available <code>function_declarations</code>, not the Python callables themselves.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">02</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Decision</strong>
          <p>The model emits a structured <code>function_call</code> part — a name and an args object that conform to the schema. This is constrained decoding, not free text. The model never executes anything; it only produces an instruction.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">03</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Dispatch</strong>
          <p>The runtime receives the <code>function_call</code> event, looks up the Python callable by name, validates the args, and invokes it. Permissions, confirmations, timeouts, and retries apply <em>here</em>, not inside the model.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="timeline-number">04</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Response</strong>
          <p>The return value becomes a <code>function_response</code> event appended to the session and fed back to the model on the next turn. The model reads it and decides what to do next.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mechanism-note">
      The same four-step shape — declare, decide, dispatch, respond — appears
      in OpenAI function calling, Anthropic tool use, and Gemini function
      calling. The vocabulary differs; the protocol is the same.
    </p>

    <figure class="code-card code-card--standalone">
      <figcaption>
        <span class="lang-pill">python</span>
        <span class="code-filename">google_adk_function_tool.py</span>
        <a href="https://adk.dev/tools-custom/function-tools/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google ADK docs</a>
      </figcaption>
      <Code code={functionToolCode} lang="python" theme="github-light" />
    </figure>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
